---
fmContentType: glossaryEntry
term: "Bioavailability"
letter: "B"
anchor: "bioavailability"
category: "edibles"
---

The proportion of a substance that enters the bloodstream and produces an active effect. Different consumption methods produce dramatically different bioavailability for cannabinoids. Inhaled cannabis has relatively high bioavailability (around 30–40%) because cannabinoids enter the bloodstream directly through the lungs. Edibles have lower bioavailability (roughly 6–20%) due to first-pass metabolism in the liver — but the liver also converts THC to 11-hydroxy-THC, which is more potent, partially compensating for the lower absorption rate. Sublingual tinctures fall between the two.

→ [Cannabis Edibles 101](/edibles/cooking-with-cannabis-edibles-101/), [How to Make Cannabis Tincture](/edibles/how-to-make-cannabis-tincture-green-golden-dragon/)
